<?xml version="1.0"?>
<failureSpec>
    <!-- Time-based, phase-driven fault specification for CppBedrock SBFT.

         Calibrated at 15,000 requests/s, four replicas in namespaces on one
         48-core CloudLab host, with the consensus window at 64 batches in
         flight. Run it at that rate; the runner defaults to it.

         Election timeouts: 250 (low), 600 (medium), 1200 (high) ms.

         The two knobs are independent and are varied independently.

         Network delay sets the healthy p95 latency tail - 167 ms at base,
         319 at 5+50, 624 at 5+150 - and a timeout at or below its level's
         tail fires on ordinary latency and storms however healthy the leader
         is. That is what takes rungs off the table as the delay rises, and it
         is what fixes the three values: low must fail at 5+50 and so sits
         under 319, medium must fail at 5+150 and so sits under 624 while
         staying clear of 319, and high must survive 5+150 and so sits above
         624. Nothing else about the ladder is free.

         The medium rung is set at 600, near the top of its band, to leave the
         widest gap above the low rung - that gap is what the two base phases
         have to fit their delays into. It is also the one value here with a
         thin margin: it has to storm at 5+150 against a 624 ms tail, and
         700 ms survives there with only 20 view changes, so the boundary is
         not far below it. If 600 turns out to survive at 5+150, the rung
         comes down rather than the tail moving - anything above 319 works.

         Leader proposal delay decides which of the surviving rungs is best,
         by deciding which of them fire at all. A timeout above D + tail never
         reaches its deadline, so it never elects and pays D on every request;
         one below it elects about once per 60 s tick and escapes to a leader
         carrying no delay. Raising D therefore brings the next rung up into
         play rather than pushing the winner higher.

         Each phase admits a set of acceptable timeouts, and it is the
         intersection across the run that identifies the three values:

           base                    all three work
           base + low delay        low only        - only 250 fires
           base + high delay       low or medium   - 600 now fires too, 1200 does not
           5+50                    medium or high  - 250 is under this level's tail
           5+50 + low delay        medium only     - 600 fires, 1200 does not
           5+150                   high only       - 250 and 600 are under the tail

         The delays are sized to put D + tail between two rungs, never to
         match the network delay. At base the tail is 167, so 230 ms of delay
         lands at 397 - above the low rung, below the medium - and 700 ms
         lands at 867, above the medium and below the high. At 5+50 the tail
         is 319, so 500 ms of delay lands at 819, above the medium and below
         the high. The base delay is not lowered further than 230: at 180 ms
         the low rung's deadline is crossed only sometimes, so it elects
         without the escape resolving anything - 225 view changes against 17.
         5+150 carries no delay at all: its tail alone leaves only
         the high rung standing, and adding a delay there would confound the
         two mechanisms rather than separate them.

         The slow-path timeout is 500 ms throughout. No phase here actuates
         that axis: without a straggler all four prepares arrive together, so
         20 and 500 ms both catch the fast path and tie. Exercising it needs a
         phase with one replica far slower than the rest.

         No phase crashes a replica. A replica restored after a crash rejoins
         its view and executes, but the committee never regains the fast path,
         which would leave every later phase measured on a degraded committee.

         netem jitter reorders packets and TCP treats reordering as loss, which
         costs more than half the throughput at this rate, so jitterRatio is 0. -->
    <schemaVersion>4</schemaVersion>
    <replicaIdType>global</replicaIdType>
    <warmUpTime>10</warmUpTime>
    <globalNetworkDelay>
        <delayMs>5</delayMs>
        <jitterRatio>0</jitterRatio>
    </globalNetworkDelay>
    <phases>
        <!-- base: all three work. No fault and every rung is clear of the
             167 ms tail, so none of them elects and none pays anything. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>0</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
        <!-- base + low delay: low only. D + tail = 397 ms, which is above the
             low rung and below the medium, so 250 elects once a tick and
             escapes to a clean leader while 600 and 1200 never fire and carry
             the 230 ms on every request. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>300</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>230</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
        <!-- base + high delay: low or medium. D + tail = 867 ms now reaches
             past the medium rung, so 600 fires and escapes as well and the two
             are equally good; 1200 is still above it, never fires, and pays
             the 700 ms. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>600</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>0</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>0</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>0</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>700</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
        <!-- 5+50: medium or high. No fault, but the tail is now 319 ms and the
             low rung sits under it, so 250 elects on ordinary latency and
             storms. 600 and 1200 are both clear of it and neither fires. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>900</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>50</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
        <!-- 5+50 + low delay: medium only. The low rung is still under the
             tail, and D + tail = 819 ms is above the medium rung and below the
             high, so 600 escapes while 1200 never fires and pays the 500 ms. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>1200</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>50</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <interval>60</interval>
                    <replicas>
                        <replica>
                            <id>leader</id>
                            <delayMs>500</delayMs>
                        </replica>
                    </replicas>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
        <!-- 5+150: high only. No fault and no delay: the tail is 624 ms, which
             is above both the low and the medium rung, so each of them elects
             on ordinary latency and storms. Only 1200 is clear of it. This is
             the phase the medium rung has least margin in. -->
        <phase>
            <atTime>1500</atTime>
            <network>
                <directedEdges>true</directedEdges>
                <precedence>global,source,edge</precedence>
                <global>
                    <delayMs>150</delayMs>
                    <burstDurationMs>10000</burstDurationMs>
                    <burstIntervalMs>10000</burstIntervalMs>
                </global>
            </network>
            <sbft>
                <proposalDelay>
                    <replicas/>
                </proposalDelay>
            </sbft>
        </phase>
    </phases>
</failureSpec>
